{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/3194-hazmat-lineages","id":3194,"title":"HazMat Lineages","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Promethean: The Created"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-04T22:45:12-08:00","id":3194,"title":"HazMat Lineages","content":"(Salvaged from the old forums)<br /> <br /> One idea that I've wanted to explore for some time now is that of &quot;HazMat Lineages&quot;: a trio of Promethean Lineages that are inherently anathema to life. The first of these, and the original inspiration for the idea, are the Nuclear Prometheans described in <b>Saturnine Night</b>. They would be joined by two other varieties: the plague-bearers (i.e., &quot;Biological Prometheans&quot;) and the toxic or &quot;Chemical Prometheans&quot;. As I envision it, each write-up would be modeled after the &quot;Destroyer of Worlds&quot; chapter from <b>Saturnine Night</b>:<ul><li>Generic rules for plagues and toxins, respectively. The plague rules should expand on the basic disease rules to include the tendency of diseases to spread (thus \u201cplagues\u201d); the toxin rules should build on the rules for poisons and drugs, including a mechanic for developing tolerances and dependencies: Chemical Prometheans should be as much about the abuse of harmful chemicals, which includes drugs every bit as much as cyanide.</li> <li>A Lineage write-up.</li> <li>The Promethean Condition, as modified to reflect the variety of hazardous material with which the Lineage is associated. What replaces Electricity as a means of healing? (Note that the aforementioned generic rules for plagues and toxins can come in handy here.) What about Torment, Wasteland, and Disquiet? What kinds of Milestones might be appropriate for them? Like the Zeka, their Biological and Chemical counterparts should have a tendency toward the Refinement of Flux rather than Stannum.</li> <li>Athanors. Not strictly necessary, as it's unlikely that there have been any HazMat Prometheans who have developed one.</li> <li>Bestowments. Re-Animator and Radioactive Affinity ought to be easily adapted to conform to the alternate hazardous materials.</li> <li>Transmutations. The Zeka get a set of Irradiation Transmutations. Their biological counterparts should get a set of Infection Transmutations, and their chemical counterparts should get a set of Intoxication Transmutations. Infection and Intoxication Transmutations can leverage the aforementioned plague and toxin rules the way Irradiation Transmutations leverage the Radiation rules.</li> <li>Mockeries. HazMat Prometheans are bad enough; what are their Pandoran counterparts like?</li> </ul>This idea was originally triggered by the \u201cProdigal Demiurge\u201d section in <b>Magnum Opus</b>, where it was describing how the various Lineages might correlate to the seven deadly sins; in particular, the entry on Lust mentioned that there might be an as-yet-uncreated seventh Lineage that's based on communicable diseases. The Biological Prometheans were originally conceived to be this \u201cseventh Lineage\u201d, the creation of which would likely have Apocalyptic results. In a setting built around this concept, the Chemical Prometheans have no place \u2014 but then, neither do Dream-born, Extempore, or Unfleshed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-04T23:14:58-08:00","id":3212,"title":"","content":"(continuing the salvage operation)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why not go with <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S-fP1zqvfcE\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S-fP1zqvfcE</a> ? Just as we have five pandorans for the Lineages, why not five opposite evil ones? Radiation, smog, disease, poison, and something similar to Abyss-hate stuff (spiritual annihilation/nihilism). Basically, split chemical between smog (and other environmental polutions like acid rain) type effects, while poison would be like those people who were fed so much poison, they became dependent on it, and could kill with the smallest touch. Disease is obviously a disease - kinda like a vampire, in a way. Radiation remains the same. And Hate would be anti-Spirit stuff. Something ephemeral.<br /> <br /> <i>Then...</i> Nuclear Prometheans were made from science gone bad - nuclear fall out. They're kind of like Godzilla in that way, a dead body raised from the dead due to radiation poisoning.<br /> <br /> People raised as potential assassins, and fed poison to the point where they're dependent on poison, and their very touch is poisonous? This was done back in Rome, at least. Its shown up in stories a couple of times as well, including in the SciFi TV series of Invisible Man. (Good example of a show with mad science gone wrong, I'd think. We even have the Deception 5 Transmutation to make an invisible man with! Just add Torment!)<br /> <br /> That's a far different story than, say, a zombie who came back to life from being dumped in a toxic river. The only examples I can think of this is really cheesy examples. The B-movie, I was a Teenage Zombie, for one. The while the toxic guy and the poison guy may have similar &quot;elements,&quot; the theme that they represent, and the Lineages they mirror, are pretty different (Tammuz and Osirian, respectively). One is a tool, the other a kind of toxic resurrection man.<br /> <br /> Then, we have disease, which I'd peg as the opposite of Galatea. Think vampire. Think zombie plague ala Resident Evil, where biting someone turns them into another zombie plague. Think Supernatural's Croatoan virus.<br /> <br /> All four have different themes that'd they fill out, different niches, even if the elements are similar at first blush. But I can see different Wastelands for each, and that's where the element really comes into play. There's no set &quot;elemental personality&quot; for radiation or poisons.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Nuclear Prometheans have already been detailed in Saturnine Night. Of the HazMat trio, they are the youngest, most terrifying, and perhaps the most visible. I think most interesting is that nuclear energy was first developed as a weapon before it was successfully harnessed as a power source, which possibly infused them with a predilection towards malevolence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Having already been detailed, they're also the least likely to need any further coverage. They're probably going to be mentioned as a reference point and nothing more, unless someone can think of something interesting to say about them that hasn't already been said.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If they exist, Chemical Prometheans are perhaps the most capable of passing themselves off as other Lineages. They are the &quot;middle children&quot; of the HazMats, nowhere nearly as ancient as the Plague Prometheans, but still quite old. I see these guys as being the most sane, but probably cursed with a short lifespan, as their animating humors are unstable for all their volatility. There are two main branches of the line: those generated by chemical weapons (including poisons), and those raised with toxic waste. The &quot;weaponized&quot; branch is subject to violent outbursts, while the &quot;toxic&quot; branch has a greater variance in humors. It is possible that multiple Progenitors head the Lineage(s), much like the Zeky, and if any exist still, they were likely born in the early days of the 20th century. Those who suspect their existence argue over whether they share a dominant humour.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the view of the Toxic Prometheans as the most subtle and insidious of the three. I also like your idea about the &quot;weaponized&quot;/&quot;toxic waste&quot; branches, because it neatly sets up a solution to a problem that's been bugging me for a while now. Thinking aloud:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Plague Prometheans are among the oldest of the HazMat Lineages, and maybe even of <i>all</i> the Lineages. Cursed as truly as the Zeky, Plague Prometheans inflict Disquiet as horrifying as the Zeky's Wasteland, typically manifesting as the disease that gave rise to the Promethean that inflicted it. The Plague Progenitor(s) notably predate any living theory of disease (meaning they actually arose from demiurges with no inkling of viruses or microbes, or even humors possibly). It is thought that the Lineage would have died out with the rise of antibiotics, but MRSA strains, global epidemics, and designer viruses have given rise to fears of new demiurges experimenting with &quot;viral therapies&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>And, like their Toxic cousins, the Plaguebearers come in two &quot;flavors&quot;: those born of natural plagues, and those created through the rise of biotech. Or they would, except for one thing: modern biotech isn't up to creating any &quot;weaponized&quot; Plague Prometheans. Yet. Hopefully.<br /> <br /> As things stand, there are no known &quot;engineered&quot; Plague Prometheans. There have long been rumors of Extempore Plaguebearers who would rise during a contagion. But they never lasted long, and they never successfully performed the generative act. Apparently, you need to be able to deliberately create a disease in order to deliberately create a Plaguebearer. But with recent advances in modern science, as well as the ability of the Mad Scientists to push beyond the frontier of modern science, it's only a matter of time before one is born and a new Lineage is founded.<br /> <br /> Extending this idea: there may very well have been Wretched-like Extempore before Dr. Frankenstein created his monster; but they were not really a Lineage because nobody knew how to create one. They were solitary monsters who walked the Pilgrimage with no hope of completing it. Plaguebearers may well be the oldest of the HazMat Prometheans; but they are not the oldest HazMat Lineage. Thus far, they are not a Lineage at all. Yet. Probably.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Toxic Prometheans<ul><li>Regain Pyros by sleeping near poisonous or corrosive vapors.</li> <li>Heal by immersion in caustic chemicals. No harm or benefit from electricity.</li> <li>Torment: Corrosive, venomous. Driven to sow destruction and dysfunction. Suffers penalties to social rolls that don't drive wedges between friends. Fond of vandalism.</li> <li>Disquiet: drives people to find &quot;antidotes&quot; for the Promethean, increase in drug and alcohol abuse, hallucinations/</li> <li>Wastelands: sulfurous fumes, starts resembling a Superfund site, birth defects, acid rain, smog</li> <li>Disfigurements: chemical burns, maybe candidate bodies must have congenital defects, greasy and pockmarked skin like a meth addict's.</li> <li>Affinity Transmutation: Intoxication, replicates narcotics, addiction, toxicity, up to nerve agents at five dots.</li> <li>Bestowment(s): ?</li> </ul>Plague Prometheans<ul><li>Regain Pyros by sleeping near sick people, or in direct physical contact with a rotten animal carcass.</li> <li>Heal by infecting oneself? No harm or benefit from electricity.</li> <li>Torment: cause an immediate epidemic.</li> <li>Disquiet: hypochondria, people imagine themselves sick, and see the Promethean as a Typhoid Mary (who may well have been a Redeemed Plaguewalker). At the driven stage, they try to euthanize her.</li> <li>Wastelands: blighted plants, stunted animals. Humid air, rich with the stink of decay. Eerie silence, people isolate themselves.</li> <li>Disfigurements: buboes, lesions, fever, skeletal. Bodies had to die from disease.</li> <li>Affinity Transmutation: Infection. Probably not a whole lot different than Cachexy.</li> <li>Bestowment(s): Carrier (signature disease, can temporarily infect at will), Humoral Imbalance (force another into Torment)... Notable: the color of the Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse resemble the four stages of the philospher stone (the pale horse is sometimes green, citrinitas...)</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div>(on the Chemical Promethean Bestowments)<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Signature drug, can &quot;inject&quot; people with a touch.<br /> <br /> This can be anything between giving people a hit of weed to mellow the mood to shooting people full of heroin to watch them spiral into addiction.<br /> <br /> Bestowment 2 could be the ability to invert the Disquiet, making people dependent on the Promethian rather than try to get rid of her. Sort of like a Vampire's Vinculum only supercharged with massive drug like health problems and psychological side effects.<br /> <br /> And of course toxic affinity: Could wade through pure industrial posion without effect (if Zeka need Radioactive Affinity to regain Pyros I'm gessing these two will need something similar.)</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm actually toying with &quot;Re-animator&quot; for both of the new Lines.<br /> <br /> Zeka don't need Radioactive Affinity to regain pyros (they can sleep in mild radiation like others in their own elements), but it sure helps.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 11-05-2013, 02:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-04T23:42:04-08:00","id":3223,"title":"","content":"Once we have mechanics for poisons and disease, we'll have a better idea of how Toxic and Plague Prometheans heal. Note that Toxic Prometheans should also be able to heal by drinking poison; and the main problem with Plague Prometheans healing through exposure to disease is that infections tend to be ongoing afflictions.<br /> <br /> Intoxication Transmutations: brilliant name! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Again, once we have mechanics for toxins and diseases, we should have a better foundation for actually writing the Intoxication and Infection Transmutations. My only other issue is with your speculation about Typhoid Mary.<br /> <br /> The notion of Redeemed HazMat Prometheans is problematic at best: <b>Saturnine Night</b> suggests that if a Nuclear Promethean were ever to Redeem, he'd probably die of radiation poisoning in short order. By analogy, a Redeemed Plaguebearer would very likely die of the diseases that course through her veins. (If you're interested in a happy ending, just assume that the New Dawn purges the HazMat Promethean of her taint; but this <i>is</i> the World of Darkness.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-04T23:56:09-08:00","id":3230,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do you see as insufficient about current Disease and Toxin rules (WoD core, pg 176, 180-181)? Poisons are pretty easy: Toxins will heal the poison's rating in lethal per lethal dose, or something similar. Plaguebearers might heal themselves by transferring damage to others, in the form of illness: touch someone, roll Stamina+Composure, and heal or &quot;reverse&quot; one point of bashing per success, and the victim takes one bashing for each.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oddly enough, I completely forgot that those rules existed. Let me get back to you once I've reviewed them. <br /> <br /> I'm leery about the idea of a Promethean who heals himself by hurting other people; that feels a bit too much like a Vampire theme for my comfort zone, and is a radical departure from the way that other Prometheans work. But I'll think it over: if we can't come up with something better, this might be an acceptable option. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Intoxication Transmutations: I see a few thematic branches. The caustic, morbid branch deals with reactive, corrosive effects, and is probably the most directly combat-effective (acids, mustard gas, explosives). The narcotic branch tends to be distracting at its weakest, and psychologically crippling at worst (addiction, withdrawal, overdose, tolerance). A third and less visible branch is neutralization, whether it be that of the chemical, or that of the target's defenses. A few Prometheans might actually fall under a Toxin's sway with his ability to defeat their resistances, for example. There might be a vector factor too, since toxins do have &quot;classically elemental&quot; forms (vapors, powders, liquids, etc).</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds reasonable. I'll do a bit more research into vectors and effects, and will report back with what I find. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Infection Transmutations: I might dig out Trinity for inspiration here. The &quot;Algesia&quot; mode of Vitakinesis seems relevant. There's so many things that can go wrong with a body. &quot;Organ Failure&quot; seems like a decent counterpart to Hundred Hands and Redundant Organ. Thematic branches might be contagion and morbidity (or, in expanded disease rules, they might be the counterpart to radioactive Intensity and Damage), and maybe a symptomatic branch as well. There's also the possibility of non-Western and non-germ theories of disease forming some outliers here.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was thinking in terms of having counterparts of Damage and Intensity as part of the expanded disease rules. As with the above, let me do some additional research here: there was a old, overly-complex game called Aftermath that had a few gems in it, and I recall its rules for poisons and diseases being one of those gems. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">HazMat themes: This is where I get critical of the overall idea, Dataweaver, so be ready.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Incoming, incoming! Brace for impact!&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nuke Prometheans represent the power of science and God and the extremes of human endeavor, and are justifiable in that alone: the sheer power of nuclear physics birthed <i>five </i>Demiurges. Disease and poison, are very much parts of the natural world, and have rarely been toyed with on the level the Atom has. It's easy to see why Nukes are a part of the Divine Flame, but how about plague and poison? I know you've thought about Extempore as their origins, but if they had mortal demiurges, what were they like?</div> </div> </div> </div>The key element here is the concept of NBC Warfare, where poisons, drugs, and microbes are created as weapons of mass destruction \u2014 weapons so horrendous in what they can do that they're generally lumped together with nukes in international treaties. Think of the gas warfare used in the trenches of World War I and the gas chambers of the infamous concentration camps of World War II. <br /> <br /> For Toxic Prometheans: add to that the drug trade. It's safe to call most drugs &quot;man-made&quot; in the sense that they have to undergo extensive processing before they achieve the potency that their users are looking for; and recently, chemistry has been creating new drugs in the lab. And then there's the matter of pollution, where harmful chemicals are released into the environment as a byproduct of manufacturing procedures. <br /> <br /> Plague Prometheans don't have a &quot;manufactured by man&quot; aspect to them \u2014 yet. But our knowledge of biology is advancing very quickly, and many feel that it will only be a matter of time before we're able to make microbes in the lab with the same ease that we can currently make drugs and poisons. <br /> <br /> This &quot;yet&quot; is another reason why I'm saying that the default assumption should be that Plague Prometheans have not yet been established as a Lineage: the ability to deliberately create a disease does not yet exist outside of science fiction, to the best of my knowledge; so the only Plaguebearers who should exist thus far are the ones who were created by chance. As such, a Plaguebearer Lineage represents a man-made horror of the future, rather than one that currently exists.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T00:04:30-08:00","id":3236,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">My main point is that Nukes are emblematic of the Divine Fire, whereas Toxins and Plagues, while notable for their influence on human history, are best known for being causes of death and sickness. That's not a bash on the concept, but I'd love to find some way to elevate Bio/Chem to that same level of awesome terrifying magnificence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah. Well, they're not going to. At least, not in terms of raw power. Toxins and plagues are more insidious threats than nukes are, and that's why these Lineages aren't as visible as their Nuclear counterparts: to use a military analogy, nukes are berserkers; plagues and toxins are assassins. Both will kill you; the difference is that you'll see the first one coming. <br /> <br /> Actually, plagues <i>do</i> have a lot of potential here: unlike nukes and toxins, they <i>spread</i>. The expanded Disease rules need something to reflect the fact that diseases can be contagious, and that you can get full-blown epidemics. You don't even get that with nukes; at worst, you get a <i>place</i> that kills anyone who visits it \u2014 but that's inherently limited by geography: don't visit the place, and you won't experience any radiation poisoning. Toxins also have a rather nasty aspect to them that's not covered in the existing rules \u2014 or rather, drugs do: namely, addiction. <br /> <br /> In terms of rules, here are some thoughts:<br /> \u2022 Drugs should have vectors just like toxins do, reflecting how they're administered. <br /> \u2022 Diseases and toxins should potentially have additional effects similar to drugs (&quot;symptoms&quot; for short) instead of or in addition to damage. In fact, a drug should <i>be</i> a toxin that inflicts symptoms instead of damage (effective Toxicity 0). <br /> \u2022 Diseases and many toxins should bear a dual rating: how deadly they are (Toxicity for toxins; Virulence for diseases?), and how quickly they act (Intensity). Much of this can be mapped to existing rules; but this should provide a unified terminology, and further draws parallels to radiation poisoning. <br /> \u2022 Drugs and toxins should also have a duration: how many rolls you can make before it has run its course. Diseases generally shouldn't: part of what makes them scary is that they usually <i>don't</i> go away on their own. <br /> \u2022 Diseases should have rules for contagion; these rules should include giving diseases toxin-like vectors. <br /> \u2022 Drugs/Toxins should have rules for tolerance and addiction (flip sides of the same coin). Tolerance protects you from the toxicity and/or symptoms, but it generally comes with an addiction that inflicts symptoms if you <i>don't</i> take the drug or toxin. It should be difficult or impossible to build up a tolerance without first becoming addicted. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd also want to avoid absolutes in the Plaguebearer origins. Extempore is a toolkit origin, so let's not rely on it solely; keep it so a ST who wants Plagueboys in his chronicle doesn't have to allow for Extempore if he doesn't want to. It could easily be a more likely proposition, but a mythical demiurge still cements the Lineage to core understandings of the game. It's only necessary that a demiurge sees potential Generation in using disease, or diseased conditions, to create life. And we aren't talking about viruses or bacteria either: before the pathogenic theory of medicine, numerous explanations for disease existed. Miasma theory, for example, says dirty conditions lead to disease, and ties quite nicely to ideas from spontaneous generation, through primordial slime, to modern theories of abiogenesis. What if a pre-Aristotelian like Anaximenes or Empedocles put their theories to the test, supposing that sunlight and swampiness could give life to a corpse, not realizing they were coalescing Pestilence into a malevolent being?</div> </div> </div> </div>OK. I'd just like to keep it such that it's entirely possible that a Plague Lineage doesn't exist at all yet, to allow for the inclusion of an adventure centered around its creation (or the avoidance thereof). More than most, their fluff needs to be heavily dosed with &quot;these guys very likely don't exist; but if they did, this is what they'd be like.&quot; If done right, this can be done in a way that the writer is implying that they <i>do</i> exist, but are so scary that he can't bring himself to acknowledge that they do. The ultimate bogeyman. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also got to thinking about Pandorans, and how similar they are in to viruses. Viruses are bundles of DNA with no life of their own, and rely on other forms of life to reproduce, evolve and thrive. What if the first virus was in fact the first Plague Pandoran? That easily falls under a rumors section, but could also guide the Plague Pandoran design.</div> </div> </div> </div>Heh. This suggests a possible new Pandoran Transmutation: a sort of Possession that represents a sentient disease infecting someone and seizing control.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T00:11:14-08:00","id":3242,"title":"","content":"(More salvage work)<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Saturnine Night</b> suggests that if a Nuclear Promethean were ever to Redeem, he'd probably die of radiation poisoning in short order.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It gives exact mechanics; with low Azoth / high Humanity survival is likely. Plaguebearers have it much easier though; two words: Caladrius Anathor.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T00:15:14-08:00","id":3245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, my shallow research has revealed unto me Shitala Devi, a North Indian goddess of disease, who is accompanied by scores of various plague demons. She is tied to Parvati, an aspect of the mother goddess, without whom no matter can be given life, and second wife to Shiva. She seems like a perfect candidate for the name and origins of the Plague Lineage, if not a possible demiurge. I like the Sixty-Four Epidemics (the Cau\u1e63a\u1e6d\u1e6d\u012b Rogas) as a potential name for the mockery, but here lies an embarrassment of riches. Another idea, that ties into the Miasma theory and swamps is that Sitala's southern counterpart is a goddess of rain. While I'd prefer to keep the HazMats humor-neutral, there's a strong case for phlegm (Toxins might be choleric...).</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know about that; I'd expect the Nuclear Prometheans to be a better fit for choleric. But as you say, it would be best to keep the HazMats Humour-neutral.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do keep coming back to them as the first of the Prometheans, given the link of disease and illness to Pandora's box. As such, I find some desire to play on Hope, whether it be some sort of false hope in Disquiet (&quot;He can cure my baby's sickness!&quot;) or Torment, or something less sinister. A really sick part of me wants to play up connections to a certain healer with powers of resurrection, but that's pushing things a bit out of the realm of good taste.<br /> <br /> Toxins have a lot of potential right now, and I've yet to delve deeply.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T00:20:50-08:00","id":3248,"title":"","content":"(More Salvage)<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The way I see it, there's more than one Chemical/Virus progenitor and each progenitor/lineage may have a different humour. <br /> <br /> However than the Humour is poisoned/infected is a more important fact than the humour itself.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say there's <i>potential </i>for one or more Progenitor/Lineages, and maybe throw in a sidebar discussion of possible surviving lines. A Toxic Lineage might be hiding within Nepri or Tammuz, for example. However, their existence is barely even a myth to the Created, and many are mistaken for the Nuclear Promethean, or written off as an odd member of a common Lineage.<br /> <br /> Another interesting bit, from Hindu lore, pertinent to Toxins: the halahala, a by-product of the Churning of the Milk Ocean (the Milky Way), was so poisonous it threatened to destroy everything. Only Shiva could destroy it, by swallowing it, but Parvati panicked and stopped it in his throat forever. Because of this, his throat turned blue, and Shiva is sometimes remembered as the God Who Swallowed the Poison.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T00:36:42-08:00","id":3253,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The earliest draft of an expanded disease system. The Infection Trans will probably rely heavily on Symptoms.<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><b>Expanded Disease and Toxin Rules</b> Diseases have the following traits.<br /> <br /> <i>Virulence </i>describes the ease with which a disease spreads from person to person. A disease\u2019s virulence is rolled whenever a potential victim is exposed to the disease, so long as the circumstances allow the disease to spread. For example, a blood-borne disease is not capable of spreading unless an infected host\u2019s blood is introduced into the blood of a healthy candidate, despite having a virulence of 5, while the common cold might rampage through a kindergarten. A disease without a virulence trait is not contagious, owing to causes that are not pathogens.<br /> <br /> <i>Morbidity </i>describes both how quickly the disease spreads through its host and how deeply it incapacitates infected victims. At the end of the period of time described by the disease\u2019s Progression, an infected character takes an amount of bashing damage equal to the disease\u2019s Morbidity rating. Until the disease is cured or remits, the character does not naturally heal bashing damage if it would drop him below the Morbidity rating. For example, if a character has a disease with Morbidity 3, he won\u2019t heal bashing damage in the first three boxes of his damage track. Morbidity is also used for determining how severe a given symptom\u2019s effects might be.<br /> <br /> <i>Progression </i>is a rough interval of time that describes how long it takes for an infection to become symptomatic. A disease with a Progression of 3 hours would be a very quick example, while anything measured in more than a day would be considered slow. At the end of a Progression, the disease inflicts symptoms and Morbidity damage, and the host may make Stamina + Resolve rolls to overcome the disease.<br /> <br /> <i>Recovery </i>or <i>Remission </i>reflects the difficulty in beating a disease in terms of successes. Ailments with a Recovery trait are cured once the target number has been reached, and will have no further effect on the character. Diseases with a Remission trait are incurable, but they go into remission and have no further immediate effect, but chronically flare up again. If a Remission trait has a time period listed, the Storyteller may choose to afflict the character again once that time has passed.<br /> <br /> <i>Symptoms </i>are how the disease presents itself, as it alters cellular function and agitates the body\u2019s immune system. These are highly varied, and many are often comorbid with one another. Most illnesses have more than the handful of symptoms described in the following entries, but for ease of use, they have been reduced to a number that does not exceed the disease\u2019s Morbidity score. Symptoms with a number don\u2019t present until the character has taken that much damage from the disease, or suffered through that many Progressions. Symptoms use the disease\u2019s Morbidity as a penalty for affected dice pools.<ul><li><i>Ataxia</i>: the character suffers a penalty to Defense, Initiative, and Speed.</li> <li><i>Confusion</i>: the character suffers a penalty for Intelligence- and Wits-based rolls.</li> <li><i>Cough</i>: the character suffers a penalty to Stamina and Stealth rolls.</li> <li><i>Cramps</i>: the character suffers a penalty to Strength and Dexterity rolls.</li> <li>Dyspnea: whenever the character exerts himself physically, he takes a -2 penalty to all physical rolls for a number of turns equal to the disease\u2019s Morbidity.</li> <li><i>Edema</i>: the character takes a penalty to Dexterity and Stamina rolls.</li> <li><i>Fever</i>: the Morbidity of the disease is doubled for penalty purposes;</li> <li><i>Headache</i>: the character suffers a penalty on turns when subjected to powerful stimuli (bright lights, loud noise, strong smells, etc.)</li> <li><i>Hemorrhage</i>: the disease inflicts lethal damage instead of bashing damage (although lethal damage may still be healed below the Morbidity rating).</li> <li><i>Nausea</i>: the character suffers penalties to Resolve and Composure rolls.</li> <li><i>Rigor</i>: the character suffers penalties to Dexterity and Manipulation rolls.</li> <li><i>Seizure</i>: on a failed Stamina + Resolve roll to Progress the disease or resist its effects, the character falls prone and is helpless for a number of turns equal to Morbidity, and is then subject to Confusion until the end of the scene.</li> <li><i>Somnolence</i>: the character suffers a penalty to resist coercion, sedatives, and unconsciousness.</li> <li><i>Soreness</i>: when health boxes filled with damage exceed his Stamina score, the character takes a -1 penalty to all actions. This penalty is not cumulative with other damage penalties.</li> <li><i>Vomiting</i>: a failed Stamina roll leaves the character stunned for a turn as he heaves up the contents of his stomach. Common Cold: Congestion, Cough Influenza: Rigor, Cough, Fever, Headache, Soreness Norovirus: Cramps, Nausea, Vomiting Pneumonia: Rigor, Confusion, Cough, Fever Cancer, Lung: Cough, Dyspnea Cancer, Brain: Confusion, Headache, Somnolence, Seizure, Ataxia Ebola: Fever, Rigor, Nausea, Edema, Hemorrhage, Vomiting, Confusion</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div> </div>OK: so Virulence should probably be phrased as &quot;Vector dots&quot;: a highly contagious airborne disease would be &quot;Inhalation \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022&quot;, while a disease that is somewhat contagious but must infect a bodily fluid would be &quot;Injection \u2022\u2022&quot;.<br /> <br /> \u200bHmm. Maybe we should use different terms for the vectors. Let's see: &quot;Injection&quot;, &quot;Ingestion&quot;, &quot;Inhalation&quot;, &quot;Touch&quot;; &quot;Aerosol&quot;, &quot;Subcutaneous&quot;, &quot;Gastric&quot;, &quot;Dermal&quot;. How else might the vectors be named? The basic rules for disease state that a given disease causes bashing or lethal damage (and possibly aggravated, if supernatural in nature); as such, Morbidity should probably be likewise qualified by type of damage inflicted. Either that, or use the Symptoms to note this (i.e., &quot;Deadly&quot; for lethal damage; &quot;Insidious&quot; for aggravated damage). My inclination would be toward the former approach.<br /> <br /> The list of symptoms should be treated as representative rather than comprehensive: for instance, there are pathogens that might render the subject blind or deaf. And once you get into Infection Transmutations, you have the potential for really bizarre or oddly specific Symptoms.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Vector&quot; in epidemiology refers to disease spread directly by organisms (like mosquitoes and ticks). &quot;Transmission&quot; works as a disease-specific term, although I find &quot;Virulence&quot; more evocative.[*]Droplet or Aerosol[*]Direct physical contact or Touch<ul><li>Indirect physical contact or Contaminant</li> <li><i>Airborne</i></li> <li><i>Fecal-oral</i></li> <li>Vector or Bite</li> </ul>Fecal-oral could be be folded into &quot;Contaminant&quot;. Droplet/Aerosol includes all &quot;kissing&quot; diseases, and Airborne differs little from Aerosol save that the pathogen can survive on a dry particle (thus allowing it to be folded into Droplet/Aerosol).<br /> <br /> With regards to Morbidity, qualifying it is fine, but I think making it a symptom (see Hemorrhage) reduces the visual clutter of a disease entry, and leaves room for &quot;wrap-around&quot; damage to incapacitate sicklier characters. My goal with symptoms is probably more gamist than simulationist. We're at no risk of comprehensiveness; rather, symptoms are designed here to have quickly-applied effects (penalties or consequences) that are roughly interchangeable (useful for balancing Infection and Morbidity). Blindness and Deafness are easily added here. The details of disease are better conveyed through description and roleplay, a point the guiding text should be sure to emphasize.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another facet to an effects-based symptom set: it can be raided by the drug/poison rules, as well as the Mad Science/Alchemy sets discussed elsewhere. As well as making for some DIY Flaws.<br /> <br /> Edits to consider: Progression interval is both too long and too short. A common cold can take up to 2 days to incubate, while Ebola can take 8-16. Separate Onset trait?<br /> <br /> A disease profile:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><b>Ebola</b> Aerosol or Contaminant *****, Morbidity *****, Onset/Progression 7 days/1 day, Recovery 20. Symptoms: Fever, Nausea, Hemorrhage, Vomiting, Confusion. Additional notes: No current treatment exists for Ebola, so equipment bonuses only negate negative modifiers to Medicine dice pools. The virus can persist in the sperm of survivors, allowing for secondary infections.</div>Sections on Epidemiology (a high case-fatality rate reduces the extent of outbreaks), the difficulties in making accurate diagnoses (like House, but without the contrivances and formula necessary to make an hour-long episode), and maybe medical horror.</div> </div> </div> </div>An effects-based symptoms set was exactly what I wanted; my earlier comment about it being representative rather than comprehensive had more to do with there being effects that it wasn't covering rather than real-world symptoms that had been overlooked. It was also more of an observation than a complaint \u2014 although a comprehensive list of effects-based symptoms just might be doable. I wouldn't worry too much about it being &quot;incomplete&quot;, though; something that's representative is quite sufficient. <br /> <br /> Perhaps there should be a &quot;Disabled&quot; Symptom to handle stuff that normally manifests as Flaws (usually physical, but sometimes mental like amnesia). Another one to consider would be, for lack of a better term, an immuno-suppressive disease: in game terms, the Morbidity checks undermine the victim's Stamina, making further checks to fight off disease less likely to succeed. <br /> <br /> Curing a disease won't necessarily remove all of its symptoms: in particular, survivors often end up with Flaws that they didn't have before they were infected; and sometimes there's actual attribute loss that requires long-term therapy (read: xp expenditures) to recover from. In fact, Symptoms might provide an alternative to the default &quot;Morbidity inflicts Damage&quot; model. <br /> <br /> We should also review <b>Asylum</b> to see if it has anything useful to say, since that's the Big Book of Medical Stuff so far. Note that treatments and cures for untreatable/incurable diseases are perfectly viable subjects for a mad scientist's Breakthroughs or even Pursuit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 11-05-2013, 03:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T01:04:34-08:00","id":3263,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd also say that there should be some very basic rules about curing diseases. Something like choose between: The body can fight it off, the body can't fight it off but medicine can, incurable.</div> </div> </div> </div>True. That said, those rules shouldn't be created from scratch: the Disease section in <b>World of Darkness</b> already has a little bit to say about curing diseases, and <b>Asylum</b> pages 42-50 have a much more extensive treatment of medical procedures; both should be looked at in crafting the aforementioned &quot;very basic rules&quot; about curing diseases. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I read through the pages listed, and the physical side of treatment is purposefully omitted. It's still all the same principle, however. It lists 10 successes for a mild affliction, 20 for a severe, and 30 for an extreme one. Infectious disease should probably be more debilitating than mental illness, but I think the scale is still the same, and Recovery/Remission is adapted from the WoD disease rules (note to self: Ebola deserves Recovery 30; few things are as &quot;extreme&quot;). WoD rules also suggest that infection is a contested action, which would probably be the transmission trait versus the host's Stamina + Resolve (+ Natural Immunity bonus). One of the first Infection Transmutations should be negating Natural Immunity (Prometheans would still enjoy their Superlative Constitution bonus, but either a higher level power or an exceptional success would negate even that). So, disease plays like this:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Exposure. Roll the disease's &quot;Virulence&quot; dots vs. the target's Stamina + Resolve. If the Virulence is equal or higher, the target is infected.</li> <li>Onset. The disease's incubation period ends, and the host takes bashing damage equal to the disease's Morbidity dots, and the symptoms take effect. The host may make a Stamina + Resolve roll to reduce the damage by one box or forestall a symptom of the Storyteller's choice per success. Alternately, successes may be accumulated towards Recovery or Remission.</li> <li>Progression: At the end of each stage of Progression, the host takes bashing damage equal to the disease's Morbidity dots. The host may make a Stamina + Resolve roll to reduce the damage by one box or forestall a symptom of the Storyteller's choice per success. Alternately, successes may be accumulated towards Recovery or Remission.</li> <li>Recovery: Once the target number set by Recovery (or Remission, for incurable diseases) is reached, the disease's damage heals normally and symptoms go away. At the storyteller's discretion, there may be lasting effects from the character's battle with the illness, especially if the disease managed to inflict any aggravated damage, or lethal in excess of the character's Stamina. Dramatic failures, prolonged recoveries, or a high Morbidity may also justify long-term effects.</li> </ol>Treatment rolls happen at the end of each stage of Progression, and is usually an Intelligence + Medicine roll (maybe resisted or contested by Morbidity). If the roll is more successful than the patient's Stamina + Resolve roll, then the patient may use its successes towards recovery instead of his own. However, a dramatic failure on either the patient's or the caretaker's roll (or both) allows the disease to inflict additional, unmitigated damage as though an interval or Progression just occurred.</div> </div> </div> </div> A line from the beginning of the <b>Asylum</b> section: doctors don't heal you; they just remove obstacles to you healing yourself. A patient who's spending his successes fighting off the damage and symptoms of a disease isn't going to have much left over to devote toward Recovery. So one way that medical treatment could &quot;remove obstacles&quot; would be to devote its own successes specifically to fighting the Symptoms and damage inflicted by the disease. Treatment <i>never</i> gives you more successes on Recovery; but it frees you up to use more of your own successes in that manner. Make the Treatment roll <i>before</i> the Progression occurs (at a penalty based on how poorly understood the disease is), and allocate its successes to fight Symptoms and damage at the doctor's discretion; then resolve the Progression.<br /> <br /> That, of course, assumes a correct diagnosis. An incorrect diagnosis, or a deliberate use of an improper treatment, or a botched treatment, results in the &quot;treatment&quot; acting as if it were a separate ailment (though more along the lines of a drug or toxin than a disease), with its own symptoms and/or damage for the patient to fight off. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">True that. We'll drop the substitution rule, although we might consider using it for a comatose patient who might be insensate to her symptoms. How about this instead: Patient's successes <i>only</i> go towards recovery, doctor's successes only go towards soaking damage and lifting symptoms. Maybe if the patient drops a Willpower point, he can roll three dice and those can be used for recovery or fighting symptoms. The rationale is that a person fighting off illness by himself will likely suffer worse than a patient undergoing therapy, while doctors can only lessen a disease's effects and pressures. Mechanical justification: treatment rolls represent things that the patient's body isn't doing by itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>If a patient's successes can't go toward soaking damage and fighting symptoms and the doctor's successes can <i>only</i> go toward these things, then medical care no longer has any way to speed up the recovery process. At the very least, a patient needs to be able to split his successes between soaking damage and achieving Recovery \u2013 though you're right that patients usually aren't able to direct their healing (and when they do, they're essentially doing a &quot;physician, heal thyself&quot; by performing medical treatment on themselves). So the allocation of the patient's successes should probably be standardized somehow: maybe the successes need to be evenly divided between damage, symptoms, and recovery wherever possible, with damage soak getting top priority followed by recovery and then symptoms when they can't be evenly divided. The doctor's successes are still restricted to just damage and symptoms, but the doctor is free to allocate them however he wishes.<br /> <br /> There might be a Transmutation that allows the patient to choose how her disease-fighting successes are allocated \u2013 in effect, a &quot;biofeedback&quot;-like technique that allows the Promethean to channel her healing energies to make the most of them. Given the immunity to disease that Prometheans typically enjoy, the disease-fighting aspect of this would probably need to be a secondary benefit of said Transmutation; and even then, it would still be more beneficial to human Test Subjects than to Prometheans. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Malpractice (here referring only to mistaken diagnoses) should be kept mechanically simple. Where it isn't the primary focus of a narrative, it should only be an aggravating influence. In stories where malpractice drives the narrative, it's more effective (usually) to start with a simple disease and give the effects of the mistreatment the full-blown disease (or toxicity) rules. I'd have a misdiagnosis in the HazMat ruleset have one or two of the following effects: damage upgrade, Morbidity increase, or adding/swapping a symptom.</div> </div> </div> </div> Sounds good. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Penalties to Intelligence + Medicine rolls:<ul><li>Misdiagnosed disease: -1 to -5, depending on extremity of the misdiagnosis</li> <li>Never-before-encountered disease: -3</li> <li>Poorly understood disease: -2</li> <li>Drug-resistant pathogen: -3</li> <li>Epidemic conditions: subtract disease's Transmission rating</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div> IMHO, a &quot;Never-before-encountered disease&quot; should be open-ended on how much of a penalty it puts the doctor under. Alternately, it and the &quot;poorly understood disease&quot; modifiers shouldn't be a penalty to the Treatment roll; it should be a penalty to (or, in the most severe cases, automatic failure of) the Diagnosis roll, with the subsequent Treatment then being penalized for a misdiagnosis. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Modifiers to Stamina + Resolve rolls:<ul><li>Compromised immune system: subtract disease's Morbidity rating</li> <li>Out of Willpower points: -2</li> <li>Unhygienic conditions: -1 to -3</li> <li>Family history of same illness (when applicable) or other precursor (like diabetes): -2</li> <li>Degenerative disease: -2, or -1 per Flaw disease has already inflicted, whichever is higher.</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div> These look good, though I'd generalize the &quot;family history&quot; line to something like &quot;personal vulnerability to the disease (such as a family history or other precursor)&quot;. When we get to drugs, something like this could be used to represent the greater danger of addiction to former addicts: recovered alcoholics often need to be teetotalers because it's so much easier for them to fall off the wagon. I'd also present both sets of modifiers as &quot;suggested modifiers&quot;, just to make the point that the Storyteller isn't <i>required</i> to go into this much detail.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T01:12:41-08:00","id":3268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If a patient's successes can't go toward soaking damage and fighting symptoms and the doctor's successes can <i>only</i> go toward these things, then medical care no longer has any way to speed up the recovery process. At the very least, a patient needs to be able to split his successes between soaking damage and achieving Recovery</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say that successes double up. Each Success mitigates damage/symptoms and moves towards recovery / remission. The same goes for medicine. The only question is how do we model diseases that are potentially fatal yet cured with just a simple anti-biotic? Maybe next to recovery we could have something like: 10 Successes required, no natural healing. Or 50 successes required, medicine counts x10.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm not saying no to your idea; but we need to be careful not to overcomplicate the rules. Perhaps that sort of case shouldn't be incorporated into the Disease rules, but rather the Drug rules: namely, there should be pharmaceutical drugs (e.g., antibiotics) that are especially effective against certain diseases. Think of it as an equipment bonus: if you have the right tool for the job, your medical treatment will be much more effective. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this one is important enough to get into the drug rules like I propsed and not just be an equipment bonus. That is it should have more effects than just extra dice. On a story level it justifies the race for the cure. It can cause interesting conflicts when somebody needs to go to hospital or else, yet has some reason they can't. Maybe because doctors will notice that they're a test subject once they do an x-ray. Robot organs are kind of obvious. On a system level it's needed to model something I think is completely fundamental to medicine. That some diseases are almost untouchable to medicine yet easily defeated by the body (common cold) while others can be easily taken out by a simple over the shelf antibiotic yet can kill you if you don't have any. As the saying goes: As simple as possible, but no simpler. If the drug rules cant model fundamentals like that, then it's simpler than possible. Medicine is just an inherently complex topic.</div> </div> </div> </div> Just to clarify: you're arguing that this needs to be specified as a detail about the <i>disease</i>, rather than as a detail about the <i>medicine</i>, right? <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T01:20:20-08:00","id":3273,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheKingsRaven</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">On a system level it's needed to model something I think is completely fundamental to medicine. That some diseases are almost untouchable to medicine yet easily defeated by the body (common cold) while others can be easily taken out by a simple over the shelf antibiotic yet can kill you if you don't have any. As the saying goes: As simple as possible, but no simpler. If the drug rules cant model fundamentals like that, then it's simpler than possible. Medicine is just an inherently complex topic.</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Storyteller can waive the recovery rolls if he's using a disease with a knock-out cure. That's sufficiently modeled enough for me, since the ST decides the particular of the diseases he decides to use. Within the +/-5 dice parameters, that's still not enough to model a near-immediate recovery, especially with a highly Morbid sickness. A possible compromise is to introduce a Symptom that provides a bonus to healing checks when treated properly, and a Morbidity kick when <i>not</i>.<br /> <br /> The inherent difficulty of a disease system is that there so many afflictions, with so many causes and symptoms and vectors, that it's impossible to write a system with both verisimilitude and playability. Not to mention a level of balance, given its significance to infection Transmutations.<br /> <br /> Drug Rules: The same difficulties apply towards medications, but these are so specialized and controlled that they should probably just be considered to already be factored into treatment rolls. The difficulty is that plenty of these have serious side effects worthy of their own Symptoms or Morbidity. I think this could be worked as the doctor's failed Medicine roll imposes a Symptom swap, as the side effects overtake the disease in terms of severity (and get swapped back at the next successful Medicine roll). Toxin rules might also be used, but since we're not likely to bother detailing C03C high-ceiling diuretics and their effects, throwing on whatever level of complexity therein should be ST's choice.<br /> <br /> And now that we're on toxins, a little disease self-education goes a long way. Both pathogens and poisons have a shared medical history, and the Toxin rules probably will diverge from disease rules in terms of the transmission trait and Recovery time. There might be a Recovery/Remission rump trait, detailing the likelihood of secondary illnesses like cancer, birth defects, or infertility. I'm thinking the existing drug rules are detailed enough to cover &quot;safe&quot; doses of recreational drugs, but working out their Intoxication effects might take some work.</div> </div> </div> </div> Also, the existing drug rules don't touch on the topic of addiction at all. That, at least, should be added in, as the addictive nature of drugs is a big part of what makes them so troubling. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm. I think we have a few options regarding addiction. We can adapt the blood addiction rules from Vampire, with some tweaks to incorporate expanded toxin rules. We can make addiction into a mild derangement and either work it into toxin rules, or sidebar it with suggested uses. We could introduce withdrawal as a disease, with a Morbidity 1-3, that triggers when a number of days go by without a fix.<br /> <br /> Addiction already does exist as a Flaw (WoD, 218), it just doesn't have a complex ruleset behind it. Whatever tack taken, I wouldn't want to devote much more than a sidebar to the topic, unless the drug rules demand further expansion.</div> </div> </div> </div> Since Addiction already exists as a Flaw, I'd want to incorporate it into the Addiction rules. I do see the rules as being fairly basic: add a rating that determines how addictive the drug is, and use that rating both for determining whether or not you get hooked and for whether or not you can kick the addiction. But closely related to the Addiction mechanics would be Tolerance mechanics: expose yourself to a drug often enough, and it eventually stops affecting you as much. However, this resistance to its effects comes hand-in-hand with addiction: <i>not</i> taking the drug afflicts you with withdrawal symptoms, and kicking the habit wipes out the tolerance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T01:23:54-08:00","id":3275,"title":"","content":"\u2026and that's as far as the topic went before petering out. Since then, <b>God-Machine Chronicle</b> has come out with some relevant rule updates; at the very least, the Addiction Flaw has been replaced with a Condition, and the business about Symptoms translates directly to Conditions. <br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T14:31:09-08:00","id":3763,"title":"","content":"Are you thinking about one disease based lineage or several? A viral promethean has a nice symmetry, what with both prometheans and viruses being borderline life, but a fungal lineage suggests some really nasty powers, and hey there's always bacterial. <br /> <br /> As for chemical prometheans, what about corrosives? Not as insidious as poison, more along the lines of nuclear in just outright destruction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-05T16:51:41-08:00","id":3863,"title":"","content":"I could see there being several thematic subsets of Transmutations within the Infection set, such as viral, fungal, and bacterial, that might build on each other (thus encouraging specialization); and there could even be multiple Bestowments representing such distinctions. But in broad terms (e.g., the nature of Torment, Disquiet, and Wasteland), I\u2019d like it to effectively be one Biological Lineage. Likewise with Chemical Prometheans and corrosives: I could definitely see them having corrosive powers among their Bestowments and Transmutations; but corrosives strike me as too much of a one-trick pony to build an entire Lineage around. (Granted, corrosives are a poor fit for the Intoxication category's <i>name</i>; but that\u2019s because there\u2019s no one good name that covers the full scope of what a Chemical Promethean\u2019s powers are intended to cover.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-21T01:06:54-08:00","id":15222,"title":"","content":"Oh I wasn't reminded of this until earlier, but in nwod there are zombie plagues, considering nuclear prometheans can raise zombies themselves I'd say the disease lineage ought to be able to raise zombies if not more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-22T23:03:16-08:00","id":16483,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/3194-hazmat-lineages?p=3763#post3763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Are you thinking about one disease based lineage or several? A viral promethean has a nice symmetry, what with both prometheans and viruses being borderline life, but a fungal lineage suggests some really nasty powers, and hey there's always bacterial.<br /> <br /> As for chemical prometheans, what about corrosives? Not as insidious as poison, more along the lines of nuclear in just outright destruction.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't remember all the details, but the notion was that each plague Promethean carried its own specific disease. Sort of like the Morbus vampires, really, but since their existence is owed to their disease, there's an even more personal connection. Infection would transmit and aggravate the illness, but the symptoms and vector would be specific to the Promethean.<br /> <br /> I don't see a good reason to highlight corrosives as a &quot;signature&quot; chemical. Even the most hellbent demiurge with the weakest sense of reason or faith is not going to consider dissolving his test subject. Although it would make for a most convincing <i>digestio</i>. As an Intoxication power, it could easily work.<br /> <br /> If it's not in that original thread, then it's on my hard drive somewhere, or my cloud, with a rough skeleton of Infectmutations.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://\u200bhttps://docs.google.com/document/d/1STb1620WyD6G14YNylqn9JGAbvGmN0VNux-idxABaxU/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">http://\u200bhttps://docs.google.com/docu...it?usp=sharing</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>LFP: Foreign Bodies (Deviant: the Renegades)","edited":{"userid":969,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","user":"Verge","date":". 11-23-2013, 12:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","name":"Verge","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-23T06:41:30-08:00","id":16610,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ve ported over everything of significance from the original thread; the only thing I didn\u2019t port was a brief digression that occurred early on in the thread. More to the point though, if it\u2019s not in this topic, it\u2019s going bye-bye with the old forums; so if you have anything of interest to post that\u2019s not here, please do post.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-23T07:10:13-08:00","id":16625,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/3194-hazmat-lineages?p=16483#post16483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't remember all the details, but the notion was that each plague Promethean carried its own specific disease. Sort of like the Morbus vampires, really, but since their existence is owed to their disease, there's an even more personal connection. Infection would transmit and aggravate the illness, but the symptoms and vector would be specific to the Promethean.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much this. More specifically, consider the way Beasts and Elementals are differentiated in <b>Changeling</b>: you select a Kith and a customizable Contract such as <i>Claws and Fangs</i>. The closest thing to a counterpart for \u201cKith\u201d in <b>Promethean</b> is the ability to choose your Bestowment; if the distinction between viral, fungal, and bacterial is something that\u2019s interesting enough to codify, you ought to be able to do so through separate viral, fungal, and bacterial Bestowments; if you can\u2019t come up with interesting and distinct Bestowments for each, it\u2019s probably an indication that the differences aren\u2019t worth pursuing. The other half of it is that the Infection Transmutations ought to be adjustable to an extent, in a manner similar to how many Spiritus Transmutations reqiure you to specify what kind of monster they deal with. <br /> <br /> The same ought to hold true of the Toxic Prometheans and their Intoxication Transmutations.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-30T12:17:09-07:00","id":102097,"title":"","content":"Ran across <a href=\"http://news.nationalgeographic.com/news/2014/03/140330-embryonic-stem-cell-virus-science/\" target=\"_blank\">this </a>this morning and it got my mind all afroth with notions<br /> <br /> The TLDR of it is this: some 8% of human DNA is actually accumulated viral DNA. Retroviruses, somewhere in our simian ancestry, wrote their own code into our ancestors' genes, which made it inheritable. Long thought to be just junk DNA, it turns out that the viral code plays an important part in stem cell pluripotency, the ability to become tissue of any sort. If that DNA is suppressed, stem cells will become a sort of connective tissue.<br /> <br /> Which made me think of Promethean in general, and the plague Prometheans in particular. Stem cells embody a Promethean theme in themselves, life claimed from life, and the potential for radical transformation and growth. We sort of get a leaning toward it in clones, I suppose, but it could stand to reason that a full Promethean could be created with altered stem cells, a rig of bio-reactors, and a little divine inspiration.<br /> <br /> And then the viruses. Beside the fun body horror trope of viruses changing your body, mind, and soul, we have here evidence that suggests that one of the defining characteristics of human life is this inheritable viral pluripotency. Is humanity a virus? Maybe the Plague Promethean isn't a Lineage, but one of the Created who unlocked this secret in her Pilgrimage, and deliberately infected herself with a host of viruses. Maybe she, or an inspired doctor, is subjecting stem cell specimens to a host of retroviruses to fuel a radical transformation. Maybe he's been rewriting retrovirus DNA to rewrite human DNA, but, surprise! That goes both ways, and he's tracking virulent Promethean DNA out the door with him, because his decontamination protocols are slap-dashed together in his secret lab.<br /> <br /> Maybe that's the secret to the New Dawn: get your own DNA. Collect all the biological, psychological, and spiritual markers, because you can't cross that border without your papers being in order.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>LFP: Foreign Bodies (Deviant: the Renegades)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","name":"Verge","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-02T11:53:59-07:00","id":104224,"title":"","content":"A very interesting idea, Verge. I like it. Gets my Promethean vitriol flowing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1LPTOgwgXBVVXxeyQKJOebzMgsN5vUl09k9fwLQVfNaw/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf: the Forsaken Overhaul</a>: A W:tF Shard and House Rules, Pre-Idigam Chronicles and taking different route. Constantly being Updated.<br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1CSomGcKor9qHrEb7nH15g29587CSzWQY5IgjCRRhdSI/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">New Bloodlines and Blood and Smoke Updates</a>: Homebrew Bloodlines and updates to prior ones.<br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nHsr9MBiPUrvZLsGDgwnEeEVsxxHFgBqJMUxcCw3BYk/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Belial's Brood Blood and Smoke</a>: An updated take on the Forsworn.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/114-tatterdemalion-king","name":"Tatterdemalion King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar114_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"241"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}